A||||8||Pretender||Dreaded Headder Install!||||1||
Z||000000||Pretender||04-18-2000||12:32 AM||||It started on friday night at 11pm. I picked up my headders and my friend and went to my fathers house to use his oven! I heatcoated my headders in his house because i heard it stunk up the place. Better his then mine...Then it was off to my friends garage to get started. I figured on only being about 5-6 hours but i was in for a surprise..<P>I decided not to drop the cradle but i think that pulling the engine might of been easier. <P>Off went the decklid and i sprayed the front manifold bolts. Jacked up the car and sprayed the bolts from under neith on the rear manifold. I was lucky that no bolts bolts broke in the heads. i probably would of sold the car. I removed the manifolds by starting with the one in the front. bolts were easy to access and easily backed out with out a problem. I tried to remove the bolts linking the front manifold to the factory y-pipi but broke a stud. i used a dremel with a cutting disc to cut through the other bolt holding it on. Manifold 1 out and appears to be in mint condition. There were 2-3 very loose manifold bolts holding it on and that probably was the reason for the whole leaking manifold. Manifold #2. I removed the top 3 bolts from above the car while sitting and leaning on the engine. they were tight! my hands were shaking because i was scared that one was going to snap. Another spray/30 more minutes to let it soak in and i managed to back them out without a problem. Under the car i went. The center bolt was the easiest to remove so that came out super easy. It was also very loose. the other two were a pain in the as* but they came out with a few words/twisting and turning the socket wrench in every which way. WHile i was down there i removed the 2 bolts holding the y-pipe to the exhaust. The air box had to be removed along with the inlet tube to the throttle bodie to make clearance to pull the y-pipe and manifold out through the top of the engine. Manifold #2 looked in great cond. also i know now that my problem was loose bolts holding them to the heads. I applied antiseize to the new headder bolts(suppied by phill) and backed them in using a torx end in a 1/4 inch socket. A few were tight going in so i brought them back out and in and out and in untill they went in smoothly and bot. out. The front ones were easy and everything went fine. The rear ones were also easy. just took my time and made sure i didn't strip anything out. The front headder was bolted on after the copper gasket stuff set up(5-minutes). easy stuff so far. Then the rear on....It didn't FIT! i'm under there holding this thing up and trying to get it to fit over the studs but it wouldn't go. The A/C had to be removed. This was a pain. Both bolts holding it in were right accrossed from each other and the only thing i could fit in there was a wrench and there was no way that a small wrench would break this bolt from being stuck. I eventually backed out the one useing a pair of vise grips. this made a hole through the bracket that my extension could fit through and finally the extreamly heavey A/C unit fell out(hitting my shoulder in the process). I figured after it was out(by the way it took 3 hours to remove) i figured that the headder would slide right in...NOPE! the studs were in the way! So i backed out stud by stud untill it finally gave and flushed itself against the head. All i had to do now was to back the studs back in and bolt it down. Stuipd things went in fine with out the headder there, they would absolutey would not back into the hole....i almost gave up but i managed to get one in. there is such and angle on them to back them in after the headder was in the was in the way. There was also no way to tighten them. half a 1/4 turn at a time using a smalltorx end and a small wrench. the bottum 3 studs were finally backed in one at a time over the period of about 3 more hours. the top rear studs were going in fine untill i hit the last hole (near the battery). That thing was bad. real bad to back in and bolt down. with the two headders finally bolted in it was time for the new y-pipe. Alot easier then anything so far but the egr valve did give me some problems. Another broken bolt. the y-pipe was bolted on and i was ready to bolt it up to my exhaust system! YEAH! BUT it doesn't match up to the exhaust system. the flange on the y-pipe had the holes where the bolts go through about 1/4 wider the then one that the exhaust system had so i had to grind out the flange on the exhaust out about 1/8 on each side so the bolts could lind up and thread into the flange on the y-pipe. After that i find out that the connection between the y-pipe and exhaust system is on a kink and wouldn't bolt up at all....I gave up and went home pissed off and ready to rip into someone...The next day i started on it again and bolted it up anyway thinking that it might draw it up but i was wrong. I double checked all the headder bolts and made sure they were tight. installed the decklid and lowered the car off of the jack stands. Put the spark plug wires back on and started up my baby....IT WAS BAD!!!! spark plug wires in the wrong places because there was an obvious miss and the exhaust leak sound worst then it was before i started the entier job 30 hours ago!!!!! that was it. i came home after fooling with the spark plug wires(still in the wrong places) and orderd some pipeing from summit to fix the exhaust leak problem shouldn't take more then 2 hours to fix with the prober hardware(clamps/bolts/new flange). The problem with the miss will hopefully be solved by you guys in the other post about where they go...Sorry this is so long but i want everyone to know that before you start this job beware because it will trust your faith in your car. Maybe the other headders available  wouldn't be as hard to install if the position of the tubes were away from the bolts..Sorry if there are spelling errors this post is way too long to read over and spell check. So ask away everyone if you have questions about the install. I will be attending a street race at the local track to see if there has been an improvement in the timeslip department. talk to you later and sorry for this post being the one of the longest in the history of the forum...L8r. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Ed||04-18-2000||12:58 AM||||WOW!<P>That's why my daily driver (Fiero) car is stock and I take it to the dealer to work on. <P>If I get a project car its going to be an English sports car like I've had before.  Tractor/truck engines and wagon suspensions. Pretty straightforward, although studs can be broken off in those too. Lots of space in the engine compartment. <P>  ||||reg||1
